00:00:29.843 --> 00:00:36.050
Our oceans and lakes are filled with copepods,
a myriad of small crustacean species that

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:39.372
might float as plankton or infect other creatures.

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:43.810
And as they’re living in whatever manner
best suits them, some copepods—like our

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:48.389
friend here—become more than just their
own creature.

00:00:48.389 --> 00:00:57.000
They become a surface, a place for someone
else, or something else, to settle down upon.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.562
In the case of this copepod, the residents
that have taken root on its carapace are many,

00:01:01.562 --> 00:01:08.677
many ciliates, their single-celled bodies
accumulating into a microscopic bouquet.

00:01:08.677 --> 00:01:13.753
And before we get into the specifics of what’s
going on with this meeting of crustacean and

00:01:13.753 --> 00:01:16.701
ciliate, I want to ask you a quick question.

00:01:16.701 --> 00:01:19.941
Don’t overthink it, just go with your gut instinct:

00:01:19.941 --> 00:01:22.276
Who would you rather be in this scenario?

00:01:22.276 --> 00:01:23.456
The copepod?

00:01:23.456 --> 00:01:24.416
Or the ciliate?

00:01:26.641 --> 00:01:28.940
Now I promise you that there’s no right answer
to this question.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:33.570
It’s just something that’s interesting
to think about whenever we peer into the microcosmos

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:37.850
and see creatures with habits that seem
quite different to our own.

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:40.664
Though we should note that we do have some
things in common with

00:01:40.664 --> 00:01:42.703
the microcosmos in this context.

00:01:42.703 --> 00:01:48.409
Our skin is covered with its own array of
microbes, an invisible community of bacteria

00:01:48.409 --> 00:01:53.302
and fungi and other creatures that affect
the way our bodies interact

00:01:53.302 --> 00:01:54.980
with the world around us.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:02:00.070
So if you were asked who you’d rather be,
a human or a microbe living on a human, we

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:04.538
would not blame you at all if you stuck with
what’s familiar and instinctively went with

00:02:04.538 --> 00:02:06.153
“a human being please.”

00:02:06.153 --> 00:02:11.067
But the microbiome on our skin has a different
experience, than say,

00:02:11.067 --> 00:02:13.231
these ciliates growing on a rotifer.

00:02:13.231 --> 00:02:18.730
For one, they spend their lives in water,
which means they have a completely different

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:23.058
environment shaping their needs and microbial
relationships.

00:02:23.058 --> 00:02:25.530
Plus, they are a whole lot smaller than us.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:30.430
So that’s what we’re going to focus on
today: what happens when one tiny creature

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:33.599
decides to live on another tiny creature.

00:02:38.688 --> 00:02:44.319
There’s a name for this kind of relationship:
epibiosis, and it’s not limited to the microcosmos

00:02:44.319 --> 00:02:46.195
or to water environments.

00:02:46.195 --> 00:02:50.180
It might help to think of it as the flip side
of another relationship we’ve watched play

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:56.640
out in the microcosmos: endosymbiosis, where
one creature decides to live on the inside

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.810
of another one, like these algae growing inside
of a hydra.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:05.629
But the relationships between endosymbionts
and their host can look different depending

00:03:05.629 --> 00:03:06.950
on the species.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:11.849
But in cases like these, it might be the host
providing shelter in the form of their bodies

00:03:11.849 --> 00:03:16.272
in exchange for nutrients made by their endosymbiont.

00:03:16.272 --> 00:03:22.078
In contrast, epibiosis is when one creature
decides to live on the outside of another one.

00:03:22.078 --> 00:03:27.565
And sometimes epibiosis is super obvious,
like it was with the copepod we started out with.

00:03:27.565 --> 00:03:30.340
But take a look at this dinamoeba.

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:36.156
This organism was first described by the American
naturalist Joseph Leidy in the 19th century.

00:03:36.156 --> 00:03:40.700
And from a distance, it doesn’t seem like
the dinamoeba has much going on.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:45.417
There’s certainly no ornate, flowery growths
protruding from it.

00:03:45.417 --> 00:03:52.055
But when you zoom in, you can see all sorts
of bacteria and archaea arranged on its surface.

00:03:52.055 --> 00:03:58.003
In this case, those bacteria and archaea are
epibionts, which is the name that we give to

00:03:58.003 --> 00:04:00.946
organisms growing on top of the other organism.

00:04:00.946 --> 00:04:07.012
And the dinamoeba is the basibiont, the organism
that the epibionts are growing upon.

00:04:07.012 --> 00:04:09.855
And in the microcosmos, 
sometimes you are the basibiont,

00:04:09.855 --> 00:04:12.166
and other times you are the epibiont.

00:04:12.166 --> 00:04:17.449
This leafy ciliate zoothamnium, for example,
can be found growing on copepods, similar

00:04:17.449 --> 00:04:20.602
to how we found our ciliates at the beginning
of this video.

00:04:20.602 --> 00:04:27.349
But we have also found zoothamnium with euglenoids
growing on them like little green sprinkles

00:04:27.349 --> 00:04:29.659
that stay glued to their stalks.

00:04:29.659 --> 00:04:34.659
And we’ve even seen strange sights like
these prokaryotes growing on top of a larger

00:04:34.659 --> 00:04:38.990
prokaryote, though we’re not sure what species
they are and what they might be getting out

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:41.138
of this nearness to one another.

00:04:41.138 --> 00:04:45.999
And given that the diversity in epibionts,
the way they attach themselves to a basibiont

00:04:45.999 --> 00:04:47.793
varies a lot as well.

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:54.830
For the smallest microbes, their path towards
their new surface is shaped by the viscous

00:04:54.830 --> 00:05:00.650
forces around them, that creates a wall of water
they must break through before they can latch

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:02.098
on to their new home6.

00:05:02.098 --> 00:05:07.589
And for unicellular eukaryotes, or even multicellular
ones, the decision to attach itself to another

00:05:07.589 --> 00:05:14.530
creature might be just be another part of a longer
life cycle, a shift from a free-swimming self

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:18.093
that ages into something willing to settle
down using an

00:05:18.093 --> 00:05:21.000
adhesive molecule to glue them together.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:27.089
And if you are the basibiont, welcoming these
epibionts might work out well for you.

00:05:27.089 --> 00:05:31.680
These new residents might provide you with
some nutrition that you were lacking, supplying

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:35.930
metabolic byproducts that are now very close
in proximity.

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:40.680
These epibionts might even help to hide you
from predators, masking the chemical cues

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:42.809
that give your location away.

00:05:42.809 --> 00:05:45.259
But there are trade-offs as well.

00:05:45.259 --> 00:05:49.830
For one, you are literally shouldering the
weight of other organisms.

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:54.089
In water, that can make all the difference,
affecting your speed and buoyancy.

00:05:54.089 --> 00:06:00.259
And epibionts can even get in the way of important
needs, blocking off the sun or impeding reproductive

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:03.000
organs from their usual functions.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.952
Meanwhile, for the epibiont, there are pros
and cons as well.

00:06:06.952 --> 00:06:12.090
Life on another creature means that you’ve
got free transportation wherever your basibiont

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:13.409
chooses to go.

00:06:13.409 --> 00:06:17.599
And these surfaces often make for a great
place to grab the remains of whatever your

00:06:17.599 --> 00:06:19.599
basibiont is eating.

00:06:19.599 --> 00:06:23.589
But life is also unstable on a living surface.

00:06:23.589 --> 00:06:31.250
When your basibiont grows or sheds or experiences
some other major life change, you the epibiont

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:35.177
will find the very ground beneath you changing
as well.

00:06:35.177 --> 00:06:39.899
There’s this disadvantage inherent to both
epibiont and basibiont life.

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:45.819
In one paper, we found the term they used
was “shared doom,” which is a more poetic

00:06:45.819 --> 00:06:50.089
way to say this, “if they get eaten, 
you get eaten too.”

00:06:50.089 --> 00:06:54.929
And it is a strange consequence to think about,
that this partnership makes its individuals

00:06:54.929 --> 00:06:56.840
vulnerable to new threats.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:03.020
The basibiont now faces the epibiont’s predators,
and vice versa, as if their identities have

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:05.607
co-mingled and blurred together.

00:07:05.607 --> 00:07:12.369
And yet they are still two distinct entities:
one lived upon, the other living upon.

00:07:12.369 --> 00:07:15.590
So to ask the question again: which would
you rather be?

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:19.089
The epibiont, or the basibiont?

00:07:19.089 --> 00:07:24.637
Or maybe there is little difference between
the two, each one shaped by the whims of the other.

00:07:24.637 --> 00:07:31.089
Because ultimately, in some way, every organism
on earth, it has to be said, is lived upon

00:07:31.089 --> 00:07:36.410
and lives upon just as we are shaped by the
microbes on our skin

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:38.848
and the earth under our feet.

00:07:38.848 --> 00:07:43.639
But next week, we’re going to focus on one
case of epibiosis where we think the answer

00:07:43.639 --> 00:07:45.000
is perhaps a bit cleaner.

